Based on the bug write‐up, the single biggest problem is that SATP (0x180) is being explicitly skipped in the supervisor CSR–strict tester (`csrtest.py`), so the SATP‐related exception never gets “seen” in S mode and we get the wrong exception code.  There are a couple of other helper modules in the same addin that feed into that skip‐list and into the generation of the S‐mode CSR test.  To properly fix the issue you will need to:

  1. Remove 0x180 from the S‐mode skip‐list in `csrtest.py`.  
  2. Make sure that the SATP CSR actually ends up in the generated SsstrictCsrS.elf, which means touching the small generator script.  
  3. Depending on how the CSR‐model is wired up, you may also need to adjust the “master” CSR‐definition file so that SATP appears in the S‐mode list.  
  4. Finally, ensure that the exception‐classification stub picks up misaligned‐load (exception code 4) instead of access‐fault (5) when Zicclsm is disabled.

The four files most directly involved are therefore:

```  
addins/cvw-arch-verif/bin/csrtest.py  
addins/cvw-arch-verif/bin/gen_ssstrict_csr_test.py  
addins/cvw-arch-verif/lib/csr_defines.py  
addins/cvw-arch-verif/lib/exception_model.py  
```
