Based on the symptoms (spurious “uninitialized RAM” warnings when reading a previously‐written GPIO register and the MTIP edge firing at the wrong time around WFI), you will almost certainly need to touch:

1) The RAM model where reads get flagged as uninitialized (to suppress the warning for memory‐mapped I/O that really was initialized).  
2) The CSR machine interface where WFI is decoded/implemented (so that WFI truly stalls until the interrupt rises).  
3) The interrupt‐generation logic (MTIP) itself to ensure the timer bit only toggles at the proper point in the WFI handshake.  
4) The tracer/net‐change monitor (so that the MTIP edge isn’t shown at the wrong cycle).  

From the skeleton list you provided, those map to the following four BSV files (in order of most to least likely to need changes):

```  
bsv/common/MemUtils.bsv
bsv/cpu/CsrMachineInterface.bsv
bsv/cpu/CsrInterrupt.bsv
bsv/tracer/Tracer.bsv
```
