│                                                                    │
│                       1) What the problem is                       │
│                                                                    │
│ The issue is that b5d1610 added a new mechanism to generate        │
│ trst_no by “resetting through TestLogicReset” in dmi_jtag_tap.sv.  │
│ However, the implementation at the highlighted location is unsafe  │
│ because trst_no is being driven from logic that can glitch and is  │
│ not cleanly synchronized. Since trst_no is then used as an         │
│ asynchronous reset in dmi_jtag.sv and dmi_cdc.sv, any glitch can   │
│ spuriously reset those blocks.                                     │
│                                                                    │
│ In short:                                                          │
│                                                                    │
│  • trst_no is not guaranteed to be a clean, stable reset signal.   │
│  • It is effectively being generated from a                        │
│    combinational/semi-registered path involving TAP state and      │
│    trst_ni.                                                        │
│  • This can create unintended asynchronous reset pulses.           │
│                                                                    │
│                    2) Where the problem occurs                     │
│                                                                    │
│ The problematic code is in:                                        │
│                                                                    │
│  • src/dmi_jtag_tap.sv                                             │
│                                                                    │
│ Specifically this block:                                           │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│  logic trst_sync1, trst_sync2;                                     │
│                                                                    │
│  always_ff @(posedge tck_i or negedge trst_ni) begin               │
│    if (!trst_ni) begin                                             │
│      trst_sync2 <= 1'b0;                                           │
│    end else begin                                                  │
│      trst_sync2 <= trst_sync1;                                     │
│      trst_no    <= trst_sync2;                                     │
│    end                                                             │
│  end                                                               │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│ and the related combinational assignment in the TAP FSM:           │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│  TestLogicReset: begin                                             │
│    tap_state_d = (tms_i) ? TestLogicReset : RunTestIdle;           │
│    trst_sync1 = 1'b1;                                              │
│  end                                                               │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│ The issue is that trst_sync1/trst_sync2 are used to build trst_no, │
│ but trst_no is updated in a way that can be glitchy and is not a   │
│ robust reset-generation scheme.                                    │
│                                                                    │
│               3) Best practices to take into account               │
│                                                                    │
│ A safe fix should follow these principles:                         │
│                                                                    │
│  • Do not generate asynchronous resets from combinational          │
│    state-dependent logic unless absolutely necessary and carefully │
│    synchronized.                                                   │
│  • Avoid using a signal driven by mixed combinational/sequential   │
│    logic as a reset source.                                        │
│  • Keep reset deassertion synchronized to the clock domain that    │
│    will use it.                                                    │
│  • Prefer a clean, registered reset pulse/level over a             │
│    state-derived transient.                                        │
│  • Minimize impact: change only the reset-generation path, not     │
│    unrelated TAP or DMI logic.                                     │
│                                                                    │
│                     4) How to fix the problem                      │
│                                                                    │
│ The best fix is to make trst_no a clean, registered signal that is │
│ asserted only when the TAP is in TestLogicReset and deasserted     │
│ synchronously otherwise, without driving it from a glitch-prone    │
│ path.                                                              │
│                                                                    │
│ Concretely:                                                        │
│                                                                    │
│  • Remove the unsafe trst_sync1/trst_sync2 generation scheme.      │
│  • Replace it with a simple sequential register for trst_no.       │
│  • Drive trst_no directly from a registered state condition,       │
│    ensuring no combinational glitch can propagate to the           │
│    downstream asynchronous resets.                                 │
│                                                                    │
│ This keeps the intended “reset by TMS cycling into TestLogicReset” │
│ behavior, but removes the dangerous asynchronous-reset generation  │
│ hazard.                                                            │
