│                       Phase 1: FIX ANALYSIS                        │
│                                                                    │
│                       1) What the problem is                       │
│                                                                    │
│ The issue is that dmi_jtag_tap.sv generates trst_no in a way that  │
│ can glitch. That signal is then used as an asynchronous reset in   │
│ downstream modules (dmi_jtag.sv and dmi_cdc.sv), so any glitch can │
│ cause unintended asynchronous reset assertion/deassertion. This is │
│ dangerous because it can corrupt state or create hard-to-debug     │
│ intermittent failures.                                             │
│                                                                    │
│ The intended feature is to allow reset via JTAG TMS cycling by     │
│ entering TestLogicReset, but the current implementation ties the   │
│ reset generation to combinational logic and an incomplete          │
│ sequential sync path.                                              │
│                                                                    │
│                    2) Where the problem occurs                     │
│                                                                    │
│ The problematic code is in:                                        │
│                                                                    │
│  • src/dmi_jtag_tap.sv                                             │
│                                                                    │
│ Specifically this block:                                           │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│  logic trst_sync1, trst_sync2;                                     │
│                                                                    │
│  always_ff @(posedge tck_i or negedge trst_ni) begin               │
│    if (!trst_ni) begin                                             │
│      trst_sync2 <= 1'b0;                                           │
│    end else begin                                                  │
│      trst_sync2 <= trst_sync1;                                     │
│      trst_no    <= trst_sync2;                                     │
│    end                                                             │
│  end                                                               │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│ and its surrounding combinational logic:                           │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│  always_comb begin : p_tap_fsm                                     │
│    trst_sync1         = trst_ni;                                   │
│    ...                                                             │
│    unique case (tap_state_q)                                       │
│      TestLogicReset: begin                                         │
│        tap_state_d = (tms_i) ? TestLogicReset : RunTestIdle;       │
│        trst_sync1 = 1'b1;                                          │
│      end                                                           │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│ This implementation is unsafe because:                             │
│                                                                    │
│  • trst_no is assigned inside a sequential block but not cleanly   │
│    registered from a stable synchronized source.                   │
│  • trst_sync1 is driven combinationally by FSM state and input     │
│    signals, making the reset path vulnerable to glitches.          │
│  • trst_no is effectively used as a reset source for other         │
│    sequential logic, so it must be fully synchronized and          │
│    registered.                                                     │
│                                                                    │
│               3) Best practices to take into account               │
│                                                                    │
│  • Do not generate asynchronous resets from combinational          │
│    state-machine outputs.                                          │
│  • If a reset must be synthesized from JTAG/TAP state, it should   │
│    be produced as a properly registered signal in the relevant     │
│    clock domain.                                                   │
│  • Use a clear two-flop synchronization scheme when crossing or    │
│    deriving control signals from asynchronous / semi-asynchronous  │
│    sources.                                                        │
│  • Keep reset behavior deterministic and glitch-free.              │
│  • Minimize changes and preserve existing functional intent.       │
│                                                                    │
│                     4) How to fix the problem                      │
│                                                                    │
│ The safest fix is to make trst_no a fully registered output driven │
│ only from the TAP clock domain, with a clean next-state signal     │
│ computed from the TAP state machine.                               │
│                                                                    │
│ A minimal and robust approach:                                     │
│                                                                    │
│  • Compute a synchronous trst_no value in the TAP FSM logic.       │
│  • Register it on posedge tck_i with the external async reset      │
│    trst_ni.                                                        │
│  • Remove the partially synchronized/glitch-prone                  │
│    trst_sync1/trst_sync2 mechanism.                                │
│                                                                    │
│ This ensures trst_no changes only on clock edges and does not      │
│ glitch.                                                            │
